      "commonError": "Forgetting that a binding wage floor creates a surplus when labor supplied exceeds labor demanded.",
      "q": "A wage floor is $16. At that wage, 112 workers seek jobs and firms hire 88. A demand increase raises desired hiring to 100 while the wage and labor supply stay fixed. What happens to the labor surplus?",
      "options": [
        "It falls from 24 to 12; higher labor demand reduces the excess supply",
        "It stays at 24 because the statutory wage did not change",
        "It rises from 24 to 36 because firms want more labor",
        "It disappears because any increase in demand makes a floor nonbinding"
      ],
      "tag": "labor_market_policy",
      "type": "calculation",
      "objective": "LO29.4",
      "feedback": "Initially the surplus is 112 - 88 = 24. With hiring demand of 100, it is 112 - 100 = 12. The wage floor can remain binding even as the size of the labor surplus changes.",
