    {
      "id": "ERQ-PM2B2-DEF-L-002",
      "q": "A household grant rises from $2,000 to $2,160 as the CPI rises from 125 to 135. Nominal GDP rises 8% and the GDP deflator rises 4%. A report uses the 8% consumer-price increase to adjust both the grant and GDP for inflation, concluding that both real values are unchanged. Which conclusion is correct, and which is mistaken?",
      "options": [
        "Both conclusions are valid because identical nominal growth requires identical real growth.",
        "The GDP conclusion is valid, but the grant gains about 3.8% because households should use the GDP deflator.",
        "Both real values rise 8% because neither consumer nor output prices affect real measurement.",
        "The grant conclusion is valid, but real GDP rises about 3.8% because GDP needs its own 4% price change."
      ],
      "tag": "cpi_versus_gdp_deflator",
      "type": "application",
      "objective": "LO25.2",
      "difficulty": "legendary",
      "conceptCluster": "prices_inflation",
      "primarySkill": "index_choice",
      "secondarySkills": [],
      "repairSkill": "index_choice",
      "commonError": "Chooses an index by familiarity rather than by the purpose of the comparison.",
      "feedback": "The grant and CPI each rise 8%, so its purchasing power is constant. Real GDP uses 1.08/1.04−1≈3.8%. The incorrect inference comes from using the consumer price measure for domestic output.",
      "aHash": "9de7ee4b566f91bfdafaa8294d67b5a078b5d244a68dd1d026075dd991628ab8",
      "canonicalId": "PM2B2-DEF-L-002",
      "primaryConceptId": "cpi-versus-gdp-deflator",
      "secondaryConceptIds": [],
      "instructionalRole": "legendary",
      "canonicalDifficulty": "legendary",
      "originalSourcePool": "index_choice",
      "originalBossTier": null,
      "composerLibrarySha256": "ca58924188abfe6a274244df230dca1128f6af5207512de9f9dd0efc09ece457"
    },
    {
      "id": "ERQ-PM2B2-INDEX-L-003",
      "q": "A $40,000 threshold at CPI 100 is adjusted each year using the previous year's inflation. CPI rises to 110 and then 121, while the second-year threshold has received only the first 10% increase. If inflation now stops and the delayed second adjustment arrives, does that establish that the rule protected purchasing power throughout?",
      "options": [
        "Yes. Eventual catch-up proves there was no interval in which the real threshold differed from its original value.",
        "No. The shortfall is $8,400 because the rule made no adjustment before the second year.",
        "No. The threshold is temporarily $4,400 below full indexation; catching up later does not remove the earlier purchasing-power shortfall.",
        "Yes. The lagged threshold is already $48,400 because each announced CPI rise enters immediately."
      ],
      "tag": "indexing_and_real_values",
      "type": "application",
      "objective": "LO25.4",
      "difficulty": "legendary",
      "conceptCluster": "prices_inflation",
      "primarySkill": "indexation_contracts",
      "secondarySkills": [],
      "repairSkill": "indexation_contracts",
      "commonError": "Assumes an indexed contract guarantees an exact real outcome in every circumstance.",
      "feedback": "The paid threshold is $44,000 versus the current-indexed $48,400, a $4,400 gap. With prices then fixed, the delayed adjustment can restore the level, but the intervening real shortfall still occurred. Endpoints and timing are distinct.",
      "aHash": "c5b21abee1696cbf5ca7eb273bfa861c5563ff73533c0b1fd033ffd2bbe8d4bf",
      "canonicalId": "PM2B2-INDEX-L-003",
      "primaryConceptId": "indexing-and-real-values",
      "secondaryConceptIds": [],
      "instructionalRole": "legendary",
      "canonicalDifficulty": "legendary",
      "originalSourcePool": "indexation_contracts",
      "originalBossTier": null,
      "composerLibrarySha256": "ca58924188abfe6a274244df230dca1128f6af5207512de9f9dd0efc09ece457"
    },
    {
      "id": "ERQ-PM2B2-BIAS-L-001",
      "q": "A fixed basket costs 9% more, while a redesigned bundle costs only 3% more. Before concluding that substitution bias is six percentage points, what must an analyst establish?",
      "options": [
        "The redesigned bundle preserves the relevant living standard, with comparable quality and coverage",
        "Households bought fewer total items, regardless of the services they lost",
        "Both bundles contain the same number of product names, regardless of quantities",
        "The redesigned bundle is cheaper, even if it provides less value"
      ],
      "tag": "cpi_bias",
      "type": "application",
      "objective": "LO25.3",
      "difficulty": "legendary",
      "conceptCluster": "prices_inflation",
      "primarySkill": "substitution_bias",
      "secondarySkills": [],
      "repairSkill": "substitution_bias",
      "commonError": "Assumes a fixed basket fully reflects how households respond to changes in relative prices.",
      "feedback": "A cheaper bundle can reflect reduced consumption or quality rather than cost-saving substitution at the same living standard. The comparison must hold the relevant standard of living constant before interpreting the gap as bias.",
      "aHash": "8178d15e1298a177a3b9cc03718a07c79a0647e54aabf02a0450e416482950e7",
      "canonicalId": "PM2B2-BIAS-L-001",
      "primaryConceptId": "cpi-bias",
      "secondaryConceptIds": [],
      "instructionalRole": "legendary",
      "canonicalDifficulty": "legendary",
      "originalSourcePool": "substitution_bias",
      "originalBossTier": null,
      "composerLibrarySha256": "ca58924188abfe6a274244df230dca1128f6af5207512de9f9dd0efc09ece457"
    },
    {
      "id": "ERQ-PM2B4-UTYPE-L-002",
      "q": "A city’s jobless population includes skilled movers choosing among local openings and workers whose occupation disappeared. A single faster job-listing service is proposed for both. Which prediction is best supported?",
      "options": [
        "Listings can resolve the obsolete-qualification mismatch; skilled movers additionally need new qualifications before any matching can occur",
        "Listings can shorten matching for both groups; information alone removes the missing-qualification constraint just as it removes search friction",
        "Listings cannot affect either group; both need aggregate-demand expansion rather than matching or qualification changes",
        "Listings can shorten matching for skilled movers; workers with obsolete qualifications may additionally need retraining or mobility support"
      ],
      "tag": "unemployment_types",
      "type": "application",
      "objective": "LO29.3",
      "difficulty": "legendary",
      "conceptCluster": "labor_market",
      "primarySkill": "frictional_unemployment",
      "secondarySkills": [],
      "repairSkill": "frictional_unemployment",
      "commonError": "Confuses normal job search with skill mismatch or recession layoffs.",
      "feedback": "The first group faces search frictions among suitable jobs. The second faces a durable mismatch that information alone may not remove. Diagnosis should guide the intervention without assuming guaranteed placement.",
      "aHash": "a4b4a97c7bf4f67e283f3eabc0ed8eaefa76185f99c7ba052324addedaaab331",
      "canonicalId": "PM2B4-UTYPE-L-002",
      "primaryConceptId": "unemployment-types",
      "secondaryConceptIds": [],
      "instructionalRole": "legendary",
      "canonicalDifficulty": "legendary",
      "originalSourcePool": "frictional_unemployment",
      "originalBossTier": null,
      "composerLibrarySha256": "ca58924188abfe6a274244df230dca1128f6af5207512de9f9dd0efc09ece457"
    }
