      "repairSkill": "productivity_calculation",
      "commonError": "Reports total-output growth as output-per-hour growth without accounting for increased hours.",
      "q": "A factory hires more workers using the same production method. Total output rises 20%, but total worker hours rise 25%. By what percentage does output per hour change?",
      "options": [
        "Output per hour rises 20%; additional worker hours do not affect productivity measurement",
        "Output per hour falls 4%; output growth alone does not establish a productivity gain",
        "Output per hour rises 5%; subtract output growth from hours growth to obtain productivity growth",
        "Output per hour is unchanged; hiring more workers always leaves productivity fixed"
      ],
      "tag": "catch_up_growth",
      "type": "analysis",
      "objective": "LO26.3",
      "boss": "Warden",
      "feedback": "Productivity is output divided by hours. Its factor is 1.20/1.25=.96, so output per hour falls 4%. More labor can raise total production without raising productivity.",
      "aHash": "10f03bf9a2b2bca331f6741a260755026a8a61a5924780117a019fa20ec7d16e",
