      "repairSkill": "income_expenditure_identity",
      "commonError": "Incorrectly concludes “$86; services are excluded when goods have intermediate inputs” in this gdp measurement task.",
      "q": "A producer sells $86 of final output containing a domestic supplier's $46 input. The supplier buys no intermediate inputs. Unrelated domestic services add $61. An accountant reports GDP of $101 after subtracting the input from final output. Which missing entry explains why this is below the expenditure total?",
      "options": [
        "The supplier's $46 of gross sales must be added to the full $86 final sale and $61 services, giving $193.",
        "The supplier's $46 of value added; adding it to the producer's $40 and services of $61 gives $147.",
        "The $61 of services should be removed because goods and services cannot appear in the same GDP account.",
        "No entry is missing; subtracting inputs from final expenditure is how GDP avoids double counting."
      ],
      "tag": "gdp_income_expenditure",
      "type": "analysis",
      "objective": "LO24.1",
      "feedback": "Subtracting 46 from 86 yields the downstream producer's 40 of value added, not total production. With the supplier's 46 and unrelated services 61 included, value added sums to 147, matching final expenditure 86+61.",
