      "repairSkill": "gdp_deflator",
      "commonError": "Forgetting that the GDP deflator compares nominal GDP to real GDP.",
      "q": "Nominal GDP rises from $715 billion to $756 billion while a common-base deflator rises 130 to 140. A report subtracts the 10 index-point increase from nominal growth of about 5.7%, claiming real GDP fell about 4.3%. What is the error?",
      "options": [
        "It treats index points as percentage inflation; correct deflation gives real GDP 550 then 540 billion, a decline of about 1.8%.",
        "It should add the 10 index points to nominal growth, giving real growth of about 15.7%.",
        "It should use nominal growth alone because common-base deflators cannot change real-output comparisons.",
        "It correctly measures inflation as 10%; the only error is rounding the nominal growth rate."
      ],
      "tag": "real_nominal_gdp",
      "type": "calculation",
      "objective": "LO24.4",
      "feedback": "The price factor is 140/130, not 1.10. Real levels are 715/1.30=550 and 756/1.40=540. Their decline is 10/550≈1.8%; subtracting index points from a growth percentage mixes incompatible measures.",
